Text folds
==========

A 'fold' turns bytes into text, one byte at a time.
Every text that comes from outside the program goes through folds: the text of a file, and the text added to a TextField or a TextArea.
A 'fold maker' makes a fresh fold from a starting text, the text that the fold sees before its first byte.

    ByteFoldStr:{ mut .nextByte(b: Byte): Str; mut .end: Str; }
    ByteFoldUStr:{ mut .nextByte(b: Byte): UStr; mut .end: UStr; }
    ScalarFoldStr:{ mut .nextScalar(n: Nat): Str; mut .end: Str; }
    ScalarFoldUStr:{ mut .nextScalar(n: Nat): UStr; mut .end: UStr; }
    UStrFolds:{
      .text: imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .line: imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .utf8: imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .utf8(recover: BadUStrUnitRecover): imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .utf16Le(recover: BadUStrUnitRecover): imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .utf16Be(recover: BadUStrUnitRecover): imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .utf32Le(recover: BadUStrUnitRecover): imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .utf32Be(recover: BadUStrUnitRecover): imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .ascii(recover: BadUStrUnitRecover): imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .latin1(recover: BadUStrUnitRecover): imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      .utf8Scalars(maker: imm F[mut ScalarFoldUStr]): imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr];
      }
    StrFolds:{ the methods of UStrFolds, with Str for UStr }

A fold maker is imm, so it captures no mut object, and each fold it makes starts with no state but its starting text.
BadStrUnitRecover and BadUStrUnitRecover have a read # instead of a mut #: a fold maker captures them.

A 'run' of a fold maker on an input:
  the fold maker makes a fold with the empty starting text;
  the run calls .nextByte of the current fold with each byte of the input, in order;
  when a result holds a newline, the result up to its first newline goes to the result of the run, the current fold is over, and the fold maker makes the next fold with the rest of that result as starting text;
  at the end of the input the run calls .end of the current fold, and cuts its result in the same way: each fold made then gets .end at once;
  a fold with the empty starting text that receives no byte gets no .end.
The result of the run is the parts of the results that went to it, concatenated.

The encoding folds:
A 'bad unit' is a part of the input that the encoding rejects; its BadTextReason tells why.
.utf8(recover) decodes UTF-8; each bad unit gives the result of recover.
.utf16Le, .utf16Be, .utf32Le, .utf32Be, .ascii and .latin1 do the same with their encodings.
.utf8 decodes UTF-8; a bad unit is a deterministic error holding the BadTextReason, the byte offset and the bytes of the bad unit.
An encoding fold returns its starting text as it is, at its first call.

The normalizing folds:
UStrFolds.text decodes UTF-8, and:
  a bad unit becomes U+FFFD;
  each U+FEFF is dropped;
  carriage return newline, carriage return, U+000B, U+000C, U+0085, U+2028 and U+2029 become newline;
  the result is in NFC.
UStrFolds.line does what UStrFolds.text does, then each newline becomes a space.
StrFolds.text does what UStrFolds.text does, with ? instead of U+FFFD for a bad unit, and then:
  tab becomes space;
  each scalar value is decomposed (NFKD), so an accented letter becomes its letter;
  what is still not in Str is dropped.
StrFolds.line does what StrFolds.text does, then each newline becomes a space.
A normalizing fold normalizes its starting text followed by the text it decodes.
No fold depends on the locale.

.utf8Scalars(maker) makes folds that decode UTF-8; a bad unit gives U+FFFD.
Each of them gets a fresh scalar fold from maker, calls its .nextScalar with each scalar value of the starting text and then with each scalar value it decodes, and calls its .end at the end.
Each of them returns the results of the scalar fold, concatenated.

Reading:
A read of text takes a fold maker: the result of the read is the run of the fold maker on the bytes of the file.

    CapInputCursorNode:{ ...
      mut .readStr(fold: imm F[Str, mut ByteFoldStr]): Str;
      mut .readUStr(fold: imm F[UStr, mut ByteFoldUStr]): UStr;
      mut .readBytes: List[Byte];
      ...
      }

TxtFile has .readStr(cap, fold), .readUStr(cap, fold), .consumeStr(requestId, fold) and .consumeUStr(requestId, fold); CapAssetRead has .readStr and .readUStr with a fold maker in the same way.
There is no read method for each encoding: n.readUStr(UStrFolds.utf8 recover) reads UTF-8 with recover.

Implementation notes:
The default folds are Java classes, so a run of a default fold costs what decoding costs.
A read and a widget call the fold for each byte and append each result, with a fast path for the results of size 0 and 1: Byte gives them cached (.size1Str, .size1UStr).
